.1","T.D.Lee.1","H.Roomany.1","M.Comi.2","G.G.Batrouni.1","B.Simon.2","G.Grinstein.1","M.Hasenbusch.1","R.B.Pearson.3","R.G.Edwards.1","K.Pinn.3","R.P.MacDonald.3","J.DeLapp.1","R.E.Shrock.1","J.M.Kosterlitz.1","R.Kenna.1","F.Dunlop.1","Chen.Ning.Yang.1","J.L.Burke.1","John.Apostolakis.1","E.Marinari.1","A.Hulsebos.1","M.Salmhofer.1","C.J.Hamer.1","R.Petronzio.1","J.C.MacDonald.8","A.M.Ferrenberg.1","V.Privman.1","J.B.Zuber.1","Jae.Kwon.Kim.1","I.H.Lee.1","Y.Y.Goldschmidt.1","A.Patrascioiu.1","K.J.Crowell.1","A.B.Zisook.1","E.Seiler.1","J.C.Vink.1","C.K.Lee.2","J.B.Goodman.1","Geoffrey.C.Fox.1","R.Gupta.1","Kurt.Symanzik.1","L.Biferale.1","M.N.Barber.2","M.E.Fisher.1","W.Janke.11","R.B.Griffiths.1","A.D.Sokal.1","C.B.Lang.1","L.P.Kadanoff.1","I.O.Stamatescu.1","M.J.Stankiewicz.1","D.R.Nelson.4","P.G.Corsten.1","V.S.Berezinsky.1","L.Karkkainen.1","H.W.Wyld.1","K.Rummukainen.1","K.Nather.1","P.J.Kortman.1","P.H.Poole.1","J.Smit.1","C.M.Newman.2","M.L.Glasser.1","D.J.Amit.1","S.C.MacIsaac.1","U.Wolff.1","C.Itzykson.1","K.Kajantie.1","P.Butera.1","G.Marchesini.1","A.E.Ferdinand.1","C.G.Callan.1","P.B.Bowen.1","Jian.Sheng.Wang.1","A.C.Irving.1","N.Jan.1","A.B.MacIsaac.1","V.Linke.1","R.H.Swendsen.1","S.Kirkpatrick.1","L.A.F.M.Ferreira.1","J.V.Jose.1","A.B.Zisook.2","C.F.Baillie.2","M.Aizenman.1","M.Marcu.3","L.S.Schulman.3","D.J.Thouless.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"fig01_rw.png","caption":"The Roomany--Wyld beta function approximants for (a) the $XY$--model and (b) the step model. The symbols \\footnotesize $\\Box$\\normalsize, $\\circ$, $\\times$ and $\\bullet$","source":"arxiv","key":"28688c5abadfca7a13491e2fcba193b7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/28688c5abadfca7a13491e2fcba193b7"},{"filename":"fig02_xy_lambda_at_1_11.png","caption":"Leading FSS of the First Lee--Yang Zeroes $h_1(L)$ for the $XY$--model at $\\beta = 1.11$.","source":"arxiv","key":"7c8dafe11db97b333ed2da58996ac57e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7c8dafe11db97b333ed2da58996ac57e"},{"filename":"fig03_xy_r_at_1_11.png","caption":"Corrections to FSS of the First Lee--Yang Zeroes for the $XY$--model at $\\beta = 1.11$.","source":"arxiv","key":"b67430f72b3941b7e3e0f79a8ea4fd19","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b67430f72b3941b7e3e0f79a8ea4fd19"},{"filename":"fig04_xy_lambda.png","caption":"Test of the Expected Leading Critical Behaviour for the $XY$--model: (a) The effective exponent $\\lambda_{\\rm{eff}}$ (slope of a straight line fit) and (b) the corresponding $\\chi^2 / {\\rm{dof}}$ versus $\\beta$.","source":"arxiv","key":"7e8533b617fb2fbfa3c8986ea6e5ec43","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7e8533b617fb2fbfa3c8986ea6e5ec43"},{"filename":"fig05_xy_r.png","caption":"Logarithmic corrections for the $XY$--model: (a) the logarithmic correction exponent $r$ to the Yang--Lee edge is shown as a function of the assumed critical coupling $\\beta_c$ and (b) the corresponding $\\chi^2/{\\rm{dof}}$.","source":"arxiv","key":"afa7a61ed4158a85ceaa18989e22a287","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/afa7a61ed4158a85ceaa18989e22a287"},{"filename":"fig06_step_lambda.png","caption":"Test of the Expected Leading Critical Behaviour for the step model: (a) The effective exponent $\\lambda_{\\rm{eff}}^{\\rm{step}}$ (slope of a straight line fit) and (b) the corresponding $\\chi^2 / {\\rm{dof}}$ versus $\\beta$.","source":"arxiv","key":"4fa068892f718cf52febfd693f08f8d3","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4fa068892f718cf52febfd693f08f8d3"},{"filename":"fig07_step_r.png","caption":"Logarithmic corrections for the step model: (a) the logarithmic correction exponent $r^{\\rm{step}}$ to the Yang--Lee edge is shown as a function of the assumed critical coupling $\\beta_c^{\\rm{step}}$ and (b) the corresponding $\\chi^2/{\\rm{dof}}$.","source":"arxiv","key":"a22ff9d2bfc3e8fb3c333eccf748721a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a22ff9d2bfc3e8fb3c333eccf748721a"},{"filename":"fig08_xy_j.png","caption":"Minimum and maximum acceptable (a) $\\beta$ and (b) correction exponent $r$ from the $r$--analysis with criterion (\\ref{criterion}) for the $XY$--model.","source":"arxiv","key":"f069d1c25990084997c1b657da910e27","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f069d1c25990084997c1b657da910e27"},{"filename":"fig10_step_j.png","caption":"Minimum and maximum acceptable (a) $\\beta$ and (b) correction exponent $r$ from the $r$--analysis with criterion (\\ref{criterion}) for the step model.","source":"arxiv","key":"d915412e231d8409261755cf12ba992b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d915412e231d8409261755cf12ba992b"},{"filename":"fig09_xy_index_at_1_115.png","caption":"Index scaling in the $XY$--model at $\\beta_c = 1.115$. The errors are smaller than the symbols. Each symbol is in fact an overlap of four data points coming from the four lattice sizes demonstrating excellent $L$--collapse.","source":"arxiv","key":"46fa053f6725af5f02853d3ad3a41060","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/46fa053f6725af5f02853d3ad3a41060"}],"legacy_version":"20180201162134.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Lattice"}],"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04xs57h96"}],"full_name":"Kenna, R.","last_name":"Kenna","first_name":"R.","recid":1003243},"control_number":415671,"dois":[{"value":"10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00642-6"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Kenna:1996bs"],"abstracts":[{"value":"We examine the Kosterlitz--Thouless universality class and show that conventional (essential) scaling at this type of phase transition is self--consistent only if modified by multiplicative logarithmic corrections. In the case of specific heat these logarithmic corrections are identified analytically. To identify those corresponding to the susceptibility we set up a numerical method involving the finite--size scaling of Lee--Yang zeroes. We also study the density of zeroes and introduce a new concept called index scaling. We apply the method to the $XY$--model and the closely related step model in two dimensions. The critical parameters (including logarithmic corrections) of the step model are compatable with those of the $XY$--model indicating that both models belong to the same universality class. This result calls into question the vortex binding scenario as the driving mechanism for the phase transition in the $XY$--model. Furthermore, the logarithmic corrections identified numerically are NOT in agreement with the renormalization group predictions of Kosterlitz and Thouless."},{"source":"Elsevier","value":"We examine the Kosterlitz-Thouless universality class and show that essential scaling at this type of phase transition is not self-consistent unless multiplicative logarithmic corrections are included. In the case of specific heat these logarithmic corrections are identified analytically. To identify those corresponding to the susceptibility we set up a numerical method involving the finite-size scaling of Lee-Yang zeroes. We also study the density of zeroes and introduce a new concept called index scaling. We apply the method to the XY model and the c]osely related step model in two dimensions. The critical parameters (including logarithmic corrections) of the step model are compatible with those of the XY model indicating that both models belong to the same universality class. This result then raises questions over how a vortex binding scenario can be the driving mechanism for the phase transition. Furthermore, the logarithmic corrections identified numerically by our methods of fitting are not in agreement with the renormalization group predictions of Kosterlitz and Thouless.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Elsevier"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"We examine the Kosterlitz-Thouless universality class and show that essential scaling at this type of phase transition is not self-consistent unless multiplicative logarithmic corrections are included. In the case of specific heat these logarithmic corrections are identified analytically. To identify those corresponding to the susceptibility we set up a numerical method involving the finite-size scaling of Lee-Yang zeroes. We also study the density of zeroes and introduce a new concept called index scaling. We apply the method to the XY-model and the closely related step model in two dimensions. The critical parameters (including logarithmic corrections) of the step model are compatable with those of the XY-model indicating that both models belong to the same universality class. This result then raises questions over how a vortex binding scenario can be the driving mechanism for the phase transition. Furthermore, the logarithmic corrections identified numerically by our methods of fitting are not in agreement with the renormalization group predictions of Kosterlitz and Thouless.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["hep-lat"],"titles":[{"title":"The Kosterlitz-Thouless universality class"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"The Kosterlitz-Thouless universality class."},{"source":"arXiv","title":"The Kosterlitz-Thouless Universality Class"}],"curated":true},"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/415671?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A415671"},"id":"415671","updated":"2023-12-11T05:11:23.427422+00:00"},{"created":"2007-11-06T00:00:00+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count":26,"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"velytsky, alexander","full_name":"Velytsky, Alexander","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1021335"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"A.Velytsky.1"}],"affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902730"},"value":"Chicago U., EFI"},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902645"},"value":"Argonne"}],"last_name":"Velytsky","signature_block":"VALATSCYa","first_name":"Alexander","uuid":"d83c7cff-6530-4409-a296-3c72937b9ace","recid":1021335}],"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"19","page_end":"1093","year":2008,"page_start":"1079","journal_title":"Int.J.Mod.Phys.C"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":23,"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","keywords":[{"schema":"PACS","value":"12.38.Gc"},{"schema":"PACS","value":"11.25.Ha"},{"source":"author","value":"Lattice gauge theory"},{"source":"author","value":"finite temperature phase transition"},{"source":"author","value":"universality"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"scaling: finite size"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"Polyakov loop"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"scaling: violation"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"quark antiquark: potential"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"potential: static"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"gauge field theory: SU(2)"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"critical phenomena"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"finite temperature"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"universality"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"string tension"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"lattice field theory"},{"schema":"INSPIRE","value":"numerical calculations: Monte Carlo"}],"references":[{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"47","artid":"693","page_start":"693","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/176227"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"43","artid":"119","page_start":"119","journal_title":"Z.Phys.B"}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/164051"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"344","artid":"179","page_start":"179","journal_title":"Phys.Rept."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/